  "id": "Compound::DB00544 - Gene::7298",
  "verbalization": "Fluorouracil is implicated in the interaction with Tyms through multiple pathways, all of which share a common mechanism of action involving the inhibition of nucleic acid synthesis. The evidence suggests that Fluorouracil, as a pyrimidine antagonist and organofluorine compound, can induce Biliary Fibrosis, which in turn is associated with Floxuridine, a nucleic acid synthesis inhibitor that binds to Tyms. Similarly, Trifluridine, another nucleic acid synthesis inhibitor, also binds to Tyms, further supporting the role of Fluorouracil in modulating Tyms activity. These findings are consistent with the broader classification of Fluorouracil as a nucleic acid synthesis inhibitor, reinforcing its potential to influence Tyms function. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of Fluorouracil in modulating nucleic acid-related processes, with Tyms being a key target in this context. The evidence is supported by multiple pathways, suggesting a robust and interconnected mechanism by which Fluorouracil may influence Tyms activity.",
